# Vantage — .gitignore
#
# Added 2026-08-30. This repo is PUBLIC and holds a prospecting tool whose
# live data is contact information for people who never opted in (DIRECTIVES
# §0, Gate A). Real data normally never reaches this folder — it lives in
# localStorage, and DECLARATIONS puts backups in a SIBLING directory outside
# the project. That rule depends on nobody ever mis-clicking a save dialog.
# This file is the guard that does not.
#
# Git history is permanent and this repo is public: a backup committed once
# stays retrievable even after it is deleted, and even if the repo is later
# made private. Prevention is the only control that works here.
#
# NOTE: .gitignore does not affect files git ALREADY tracks. prm_data.json is
# tracked on purpose — it is the four fictional seed contacts (Jane Smith,
# Alex Rivera, Sarah Chen, Marcus Vance, all on 555 numbers), not real data.

# --- Backup and export artifacts (the actual risk) -------------------------
# The ZIP backup bundle and the per-entity CSVs it contains. These hold the
# real database: names, emails, phones, LinkedIn URLs and private notes.
*.zip
prm_*.csv
vantage_backup*
*_backup.csv
*_backup.json

# Local snapshots (Tier 1 recovery). These belong outside the project folder
# per DECLARATIONS; this catches the case where one is pointed here anyway.
#
# GAP CLOSED 2026-09-02. The dated pattern below has no leading wildcard, so it
# matches a WHOLE filename -- and the snapshotter does not write whole filenames
# in that shape. Real files look like:
#
#     vantage_snapshot_2026-09-02_121548.json
#
# which the dated pattern does NOT match, because of the "vantage_snapshot_"
# prefix. So every snapshot was unignored, in a PUBLIC repo, and a single
# mis-clicked save dialog -- the exact scenario this file's header describes --
# would have committed the full database: names, emails, phones, LinkedIn URLs
# and private notes on people who never opted in. Git history is permanent.
#
# Both patterns are kept. The prefix rule is the one that fires today; the
# dated rule stays for any file saved without the prefix, and now carries a
# leading * so it matches anywhere in the name rather than only as the whole of
# it. Belt and braces, because the cost of a miss here is unrecoverable.
snapshots/
vantage_snapshot*
*[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]_[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9].json

# --- Secrets (DIRECTIVES §4: never committed) ------------------------------
# Nothing needs these today. Phase 2 adds Firebase config, which is when this
# section starts earning its place.
.env
.env.*
*.key
*.pem
firebase-adminsdk*.json
serviceAccountKey.json

# --- Local tooling and OS cruft --------------------------------------------
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
